  "response": "Attribution of an abrupt regional sea surface temperature shift is a statistical exercise before it is a dynamical one, since no single record can by itself separate a circulation fluctuation from a forced trend. Pre-industrial control simulations in CMIP6 provide the baseline estimate of unforced internal variability, an arrangement brought about by the roughly 60-to-70-year characteristic timescale of Atlantic Multidecadal Variability. Against that benchmark the amplitude, spatial footprint and persistence of the observed anomaly can be judged quantitatively rather than impressionistically.\n\nSingle-forcing model experiments isolate the separate contributions of greenhouse gases, aerosols and volcanic eruptions, and the availability of those isolated responses has given rise to optimal fingerprinting, which compares the observed spatial pattern of change against model-simulated responses to individual forcings. Anthropogenic sulfate aerosols exert a cooling influence on the North Atlantic surface. That surface cooling, concentrated downstream of the industrial sulfur plume, resulted in the development of a warming hole in the subpolar North Atlantic, whose shape and seasonality are the sharpest diagnostic the pattern method offers.\n\nThe dynamical side of the problem rests on direct transport measurement. The RAPID-MOCHA mooring array has monitored Atlantic overturning transport at 26.5\u00b0N since 2004. The RAPID-MOCHA array was deployed across the Atlantic at 41\u00b0N in 1992; those two deployment histories cannot both be true, and the transport series cannot be interpreted until it is settled which latitude and which starting year are the real ones. A decade after the 26.5\u00b0N moorings came online, the OSNAP array began measuring overturning circulation in the subpolar North Atlantic. The IPCC Sixth Assessment Report, by contrast, stated with high confidence that the Atlantic overturning circulation weakened over the twentieth century, a span far longer than either array covers.\n\nWhere moorings and proxies point in opposite directions, the reconciliation is forensic before it is interpretive. At least one of two defects is present in the two archives: the moored instrument record contained an uncorrected sensor drift, or the proxy reconstruction carried a systematic calibration offset, and perhaps both at once. Even granting that drift in the moored data, the observed regional sea surface temperature shift exceeded the range of internal variability in the model ensemble.\n\nThe ensemble comparison admits only two outcomes, of which exactly one can hold and neither can be avoided: either the observed shift lies beyond the simulated envelope of unforced behaviour, or the observed regional sea surface temperature shift fell within the range of internal variability in the model ensemble, with no third verdict available. A defensible attribution statement is issued only once the corrected instrumental series, the aerosol fingerprint and the overturning observations have been brought into a single consistent account.",
